        "created_at": 1794390065.804116
      },
      "rev_n_11_42c9be_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_42c9be_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_42c9be",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a different formula or approach to determine the minimum number of packages. For instance, iterate through possible package sizes and check which one gives the minimum number of packages needed to reach exactly n shovels.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1402072538258100716",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390065.80413
      },
      "rev_n_12_c2fd23_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_c2fd23_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_c2fd23",
        "thought_text": "Implement a binary search approach to efficiently find the minimum number of packages for each test case. This could potentially reduce the time complexity and improve accuracy.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1402072538258100716",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390065.8041348
      },
      "rev_n_13_97355f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_97355f_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_97355f",
        "thought_text": "Instead of calculating the GCD between n and each possible package size from 1 to k, we can directly determine the minimum number of packages needed by dividing n by each possible package size and taking the ceiling of the division result. This approach avoids unnecessary computations and should be more efficient.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2429462139602806862",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390086.224504
      },
      "rev_n_14_5e7430_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_5e7430_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_5e7430",
        "thought_text": "Consider that the optimal package size is the largest divisor of n that is less than or equal to k. By iterating through possible package sizes from k down to 1, we can quickly find the optimal size without checking every single number up to n. This method reduces the number of iterations significantly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2429462139602806862",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390086.224653
      },
      "rev_n_15_4732e6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_4732e6_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_4732e6",
        "thought_text": "Implement a binary search to find the largest package size that divides n evenly and is less than or equal to k. This approach leverages the sorted nature of possible package sizes and can efficiently narrow down the optimal size, reducing the time complexity compared to a linear search.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2429462139602806862",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390086.224671
      },
      "rev_n_16_0c1f88_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_16_0c1f88_0",
        "node_id": "n_16_0c1f88",
        "thought_text": "Optimize the GCD calculation by precomputing GCDs for all possible pairs (n, i) where i ranges from 1 to k. This reduces repeated calculations for each test case.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9079751957078184770",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390118.861144
      },
      "rev_n_17_7d75ee_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_17_7d75ee_0",
        "node_id": "n_17_7d75ee",
        "thought_text": "Instead of calculating GCDs, consider using a simpler approach where for each test case, determine the largest package size i (where 1 <= i <= k) that divides n evenly. The result would be n / i.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9079751957078184770",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794390118.861228
      },
